1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">Color is a powerful cue that convolutional networks
readily exploit for object recognition,</font>

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:11,901
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it becomes a liability when the colors seen at
test time differ from those in training.</font>

3
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:16,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces Color Equivariant
Convolutions, or CEConvs,</font>

4
00:00:16,081 --> 00:00:21,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new building block that shares shape features
across the color spectrum while preserving</font>

5
00:00:21,758 --> 00:00:23,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">discriminative color information.</font>

6
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">By hard-wiring parameter sharing over discrete hue shifts,</font>

7
00:00:27,499 --> 00:00:34,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">CEConvs let networks like ResNets generalize to
underrepresented colors and stay robust to test-time hue shifts,</font>

8
00:00:34,564 --> 00:00:36,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">without throwing color away.</font>

9
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:41,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">Convolutional neural networks lean heavily on color
to recognize objects,</font>

10
00:00:41,394 --> 00:00:45,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">but real-world data rarely contains every color a
class can take.</font>

11
00:00:45,635 --> 00:00:50,514
<font color="#FFFFFF">When a model trained mostly on red cars sees a blue
one, accuracy collapses.</font>

12
00:00:50,594 --> 00:00:52,929
<font color="#FFFFFF">The classic remedy, color invariance,</font>

13
00:00:53,009 --> 00:00:56,126
<font color="#FFFFFF">sidesteps the problem by removing color entirely,</font>

14
00:00:56,206 --> 00:00:59,193
<font color="#FFFFFF">but that throws away a genuinely useful signal.</font>

15
00:00:59,273 --> 00:01:05,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper frames the real challenge as keeping
color information while still generalizing</font>

16
00:01:05,081 --> 00:01:08,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">across colors that were rare or absent during training.</font>

17
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:15,096
<font color="#FFFFFF">Group equivariant convolutions taught networks to
share parameters across rotations and flips,</font>

18
00:01:15,176 --> 00:01:19,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">dramatically improving data efficiency for
geometric transformations.</font>

19
00:01:19,732 --> 00:01:24,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yet photometric changes, such as shifts in hue, had
been left aside.</font>

20
00:01:24,222 --> 00:01:29,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">Studies of trained CNNs show that early layers
learn strongly color-selective neurons,</font>

21
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,517
<font color="#FFFFFF">which suggests color is a natural axis for equivariance.</font>

22
00:01:33,597 --> 00:01:39,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">This motivates treating a hue shift the same way
prior work treated a rotation: as a symmetry</font>

23
00:01:39,738 --> 00:01:42,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">the network should respect by design,</font>

24
00:01:42,181 --> 00:01:45,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">rather than something it must relearn from data.</font>

25
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:49,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core contribution is the Color Equivariant
Convolution,</font>

26
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new layer that hard-wires parameter sharing over
hue shifts.</font>

27
00:01:53,074 --> 00:01:58,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">It shares shape information across the color
spectrum while keeping color in a dedicated</font>

28
00:01:58,474 --> 00:02:00,541
<font color="#FFFFFF">group dimension of the feature map.</font>

29
00:02:00,621 --> 00:02:04,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because it is formulated in the language of
symmetry groups,</font>

30
00:02:04,303 --> 00:02:09,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">it slots directly into standard networks like
ResNet with no architectural surgery.</font>

31
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:10,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors demonstrate,</font>

32
00:02:10,868 --> 00:02:14,776
<font color="#FFFFFF">through both controlled toy experiments and
realistic benchmarks,</font>

33
00:02:14,856 --> 00:02:19,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">that this design improves robustness to color
shifts between training and testing and</font>

34
00:02:20,072 --> 00:02:22,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">works hand in hand with color augmentation.</font>

35
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:27,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">A hue shift in HSV space becomes, in RGB space,</font>

36
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:31,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">a rotation around the gray diagonal from black to white.</font>

37
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:36,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors formalize this as the group H-n of n
discrete rotations about that diagonal,</font>

38
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">a subgroup of all three-dimensional rotations.</font>

39
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:44,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">A Color Equivariant Convolution correlates the
input with hue-rotated copies of each filter,</font>

40
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:49,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing feature maps that carry an extra
dimension indexing the hue rotation.</font>

41
00:02:49,470 --> 00:02:53,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">In hidden layers,</font>

42
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:58,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">filters are cyclically permuted across this
dimension so equivariance is preserved throughout the network.</font>

43
00:02:58,290 --> 00:03:02,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the extra dimension multiplies the number
of feature maps,</font>

44
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:07,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors decompose filters into spatial and
pointwise components and offer hybrid variants</font>

45
00:03:07,110 --> 00:03:11,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">that use color equivariance only in the early,</font>

46
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:15,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">most color-selective stages, keeping parameter and
compute cost in check.</font>

47
00:03:16,230 --> 00:03:19,702
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation spans two scales.</font>

48
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:23,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

49
00:03:23,334 --> 00:03:26,805
<font color="#FFFFFF">two synthetic MNIST variants isolate the
phenomenon: a long-tailed ColorMNIST with strong class imbalance,</font>

50
00:03:26,885 --> 00:03:30,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a biased ColorMNIST where each class has a
characteristic hue with tunable spread.</font>

51
00:03:30,437 --> 00:03:33,909
<font color="#FFFFFF">Then, eight standard image classification benchmarks,</font>

52
00:03:33,989 --> 00:03:37,461
<font color="#FFFFFF">from CIFAR and STL-10 to Flowers-102, Stanford
Cars, and ImageNet,</font>

53
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:41,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">test the method in realistic settings.</font>

54
00:03:41,093 --> 00:03:44,565
<font color="#FFFFFF">To probe robustness,</font>

55
00:03:44,645 --> 00:03:48,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">every test image is re-rendered under a gradual hue
shift from minus one-hundred-eighty</font>

56
00:03:48,196 --> 00:03:51,668
<font color="#FFFFFF">to plus one-hundred-eighty degrees,</font>

57
00:03:51,748 --> 00:03:55,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">and accuracy is averaged across the full sweep.</font>

58
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:59,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is robustness without a cost
to clean accuracy.</font>

59
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:03,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the original, unshifted test sets,</font>

60
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">color equivariant ResNets perform on par with
vanilla ResNets.</font>

61
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:11,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">But when the test images are hue-shifted, the gap
opens dramatically.</font>

62
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:15,420
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Flowers-102,</font>

63
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">average accuracy across hue shifts jumps from about
thirteen percent for the baseline</font>

64
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:23,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">to thirty-three percent for the fully equivariant model,</font>

65
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:27,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">and similar gains appear on CIFAR-100 and Stanford Cars.</font>

66
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:31,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the controlled long-tailed experiment the
equivariant network reaches ninety-one percent</font>

67
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:35,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">against the baseline's seventy-two percent,</font>

68
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:39,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the biggest improvements exactly on the rare
classes that shape sharing is meant to help.</font>

69
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:44,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations clarify the design choices.</font>

70
00:04:44,209 --> 00:04:48,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">Increasing the number of discrete hue rotations
makes the network more robust to test-time hue shifts,</font>

71
00:04:48,738 --> 00:04:53,187
<font color="#FFFFFF">though it slightly reduces capacity because
channels must shrink to keep parameters fixed.</font>

72
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:57,716
<font color="#FFFFFF">Group coset pooling turns out to be the mechanism
that yields hue invariance;</font>

73
00:04:57,796 --> 00:05:02,245
<font color="#FFFFFF">remove it, and the network behaves like a regular one.</font>

74
00:05:02,325 --> 00:05:06,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally,</font>

75
00:05:06,854 --> 00:05:11,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">color equivariance and color-jitter augmentation
are complementary: an equivariant network</font>

76
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:15,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">needs a lower intensity of augmentation to reach
the same robustness.</font>

77
00:05:15,912 --> 00:05:20,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">A color-selectivity analysis further explains when
the method helps,</font>

78
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:24,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that datasets with more color-selective
neurons benefit from equivariance up to later stages.</font>

79
00:05:25,270 --> 00:05:27,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

80
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:31,325
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Flowers-102 under hue shifts, accuracy nearly triples,</font>

81
00:05:31,405 --> 00:05:33,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">from thirteen to thirty-three percent.</font>

82
00:05:33,996 --> 00:05:41,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the long-tailed color experiment the equivariant
network gains almost twenty points over the baseline.</font>

83
00:05:41,154 --> 00:05:48,096
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-100 improves by fifteen points and Stanford
Cars by roughly thirteen points on hue-shifted tests.</font>

84
00:05:48,176 --> 00:05:51,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">And all of this comes at a modest compute overhead,</font>

85
00:05:51,652 --> 00:05:57,639
<font color="#FFFFFF">since the filter decomposition keeps the increase
in operations and parameters to a small</font>

86
00:05:57,719 --> 00:06:00,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">factor of the number of hue rotations.</font>

87
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:06,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that color deserves the same
equivariance treatment that rotations</font>

88
00:06:06,088 --> 00:06:08,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">and translations have long enjoyed.</font>

89
00:06:08,243 --> 00:06:13,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of choosing between exploiting color and
being robust to color changes,</font>

90
00:06:13,105 --> 00:06:16,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">Color Equivariant Convolutions let a network do both,</font>

91
00:06:16,368 --> 00:06:22,197
<font color="#FFFFFF">by sharing shape information across the color
spectrum while keeping color in its own dimension.</font>

92
00:06:22,277 --> 00:06:26,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">The block plugs into standard architectures, plays
well with augmentation,</font>

93
00:06:26,832 --> 00:06:30,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">and delivers its largest gains precisely where
color matters most,</font>

94
00:06:30,895 --> 00:06:34,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">offering a practical route to color-robust recognition.</font>
